# ⏳ Time & Work + Pipes & Cisterns

Today we'll learn the **work-rate method**. Once you understand this, most Time & Work questions become simple equations.

---

# 1️⃣ The Core Idea

If a person can finish a job in:

$$
10\text{ days}
$$

then their work done in **1 day** is:

$$
\boxed{\frac{1}{10}}
$$

So:

> **1 day's work = 1 / total days**

This is the foundation of the entire topic.

---

# 2️⃣ Work Rate

Suppose A completes a job in 5 days.

A's rate:

$$
\frac15
$$

Suppose B completes it in 10 days.

B's rate:

$$
\frac1{10}
$$

If they work together:

$$
\frac15+\frac1{10}
$$

$$
=\frac2{10}+\frac1{10}
$$

$$
=\frac3{10}
$$

Therefore, combined time:

$$
\frac{1}{3/10}
$$

$$
\boxed{\frac{10}{3}\text{ days}}
$$

≈ **3.33 days**

---

# 3️⃣ Golden Formula ⭐

If A takes \(x\) days and B takes \(y\) days:

$$
\boxed{
Combined\ Time=\frac{xy}{x+y}
}
$$

Example:

A = 6 days
B = 12 days

$$
\frac{6\times12}{6+12}
$$

$$
=\frac{72}{18}
$$

$$
\boxed4\text{ days}
$$

---

# 4️⃣ Why Does This Work?

Because:

$$
Rate=\frac1{Time}
$$

So:

$$
Combined\ Rate=\frac1x+\frac1y
$$

Then:

$$
Time=\frac1{Combined\ Rate}
$$

This is why the shortcut works.

---

# 5️⃣ Work Units Method 🔥

This is often even easier.

Suppose:

A = 10 days
B = 15 days

Take total work as:

$$
LCM(10,15)=30
$$

Then:

### A's efficiency

$$
30/10=3\ units/day
$$

### B's efficiency

$$
30/15=2\ units/day
$$

Together:

$$
3+2=5
$$

Time:

$$
30/5
$$

$$
\boxed6\text{ days}
$$

---

# 6️⃣ Why Use LCM?

It avoids fractions.

Instead of:

$$
\frac1{10}+\frac1{15}
$$

we say:

```text
Total work = 30 units

A → 3 units/day
B → 2 units/day
```

Much easier for MCQs.

---

# 🧠 7️⃣ Efficiency

Suppose:

A completes a job in 10 days.

B completes it in 20 days.

A is faster.

Efficiency is inversely proportional to time:

$$
Efficiency\propto\frac1{Time}
$$

Therefore:

$$
A:B=\frac1{10}:\frac1{20}
$$

Multiply by 20:

$$
\boxed{A:B=2:1}
$$

So:

> A is **twice as efficient** as B.

---

# 8️⃣ Efficiency vs Time

🔥 Remember:

```text
Efficiency ↑
     ↓
Time ↓
```

If A is twice as efficient as B:

$$
\boxed{T_A:T_B=1:2}
$$

---

# 9️⃣ Percentage Efficiency

Suppose A is 25% more efficient than B.

Take:

$$
B=100
$$

Then:

$$
A=125
$$

Since time is inversely proportional to efficiency:

$$
T_A:T_B=100:125
$$

$$
=4:5
$$

Therefore A takes:

$$
\boxed{20\%\text{ less time}}
$$

🔥 Same inverse-percentage logic we learned in speed.

